    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. but i'm a bit confused about the 'blocking' part in DC. if the capacitor is just sitting there, why does it eventually act like an open circuit? like, what's actually happening to the movement of the electrons when the steady state is reached?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a capacitor is fully charged in a DC circuit, the charge on its plates becomes equivalent to a source of voltage that opposes the applied voltage, effectively reducing the net voltage across the circuit. At steady state, there's no net current flowing through the circuit because electrons from the positive plate of the capacitor are being repelled back to the negative plate, creating a barrier to the flow of current. This barrier is what we experience as a high resistance or open circuit in the circuit.",
